changelog: |
  0.5.0 — markdown-oneline.yml checks out the pull request's head by SHA
  (`head.sha || github.sha`), not by branch name (`head.ref || github.ref`). A
  bot PR on auto-merge (Dependabot, the hub's JEV numbering) merges seconds
  after its last push and GitHub deletes its branch before this job starts, so
  fetching the branch failed checkout after ~35s of retries: a red X on a PR
  that had already merged, with nothing wrong in its markdown. The SHA stays
  reachable through refs/pull/N/head. The repair step now also runs
  `git ls-remote --exit-code --heads origin refs/heads/$HEAD_REF` before it
  commits, and exits 0 when the branch is gone: pushing would recreate a merged
  branch, and the push-to-default-branch run reports any prose instead. Same
  two hunks as the hub's own copy (bamr87/bamr87#846).
  archive/markdown-oneline-0.4.0.yml is the outgoing shape. 0.5.0 also adds
  archive/markdown-oneline-0.3.0.yml, the shape #170 seeded, which opens with
  a blank line: 5c648c43 dropped that line from the template on 2026-09-13
  without archiving it, so the ten copies still on it (bamr87.github.io,
  bash-365.com, djangoerp, githubai, law-ai, lifehacker.dev, scripts,
  vs-sonic-pi, zer0-CMS, zer0-mistakes) read as hand-modified and --upgrade
  skipped them. Survey of the default branches on 2026-11-15: 11 machine seeds
  carry the bug and --upgrade now reaches all of them (10 on 0.3.0, bashcrawl
  on 0.4.0); zpl-viewer is on the check-only 0.2.0 shape, which has no `ref:`;
  README, it-journey and gitorio carry the bug in deliberately hand-edited
  copies (local excludes, a branch filter) and need the two hunks by hand;
  fredgar-ai's hand-edited copy is check-only and unaffected.
  test_prose_kit.py holds the template to this: no checkout by branch name,
  the gone-branch guard before the commit, every archived shape distinct.
  0.4.0 — the vendored tools/unwrap-prose.py unwraps list items. A wrapped
  item's indented continuation lines join the item line (a flush-left line
  after an item stays put: in a raw-read prompt it is a new instruction), and a later
  indented paragraph of an item is joined in place with its indent kept. The
  0.3 tool stripped that indent, which moved an item's second paragraph out of
  its list. Fixture-tested in tools/test_unwrap_prose.py. Re-vendoring makes a
  repo's --check fail once on its hard-wrapped list items; --write fixes them.
  0.3.0 — the gate is SELF-HEALING. It was check-only in all 34 deployed
  copies: it ran --check, printed "Run: unwrap-prose.py --write", and exited 1
  with the fixer sitting vendored in the same checkout. Across the fleet's last
  25 runs per repo that produced 50 red builds and 50 human round-trips over
  whitespace, 13 of them on commits authored by agents inside Actions that no
  local hook can ever reach. Now a pull request unwraps and pushes the repair
  to its own branch, using GITHUB_TOKEN precisely BECAUSE GitHub fires no
  workflow events for refs it pushes — the repair costs zero extra CI runs.
  Fork PRs and direct pushes to the default branch still fail with the message
  (a read-only token, and the house rule against bare pushes to a protected
  branch). archive/markdown-oneline-0.2.0.yml preserves the check-only shape so
  --upgrade recognizes the deployed copies as machine-seeded.
  0.2.0 — markdown-oneline.yml floats actions/checkout and actions/setup-python
  to @v7. The fleet was ALREADY there: a mechanical action bump reached the 30
  deployed copies while this template stayed pinned at checkout@v4 /
  setup-python@v5, so any re-fanout would have silently downgraded 30 repos two
  major versions. archive/markdown-oneline-0.1.0.yml preserves the v4/v5 shape
  so `--upgrade` recognizes the two repos still on it (wtd, zpl-viewer) as
  machine-seeded and refreshes them, while hand-modified copies (it-journey
  adds generated-path excludes) are left alone.
  0.1.0 — initial kit: the Liquid-safe one-paragraph-per-line CI gate.
notes: |
  Deliberately NOT stamped with a `# kit:` comment the way agent-context is.
  Identity is established by byte-comparison against this template and the
  archive/ shapes, so bumping the kit costs the fleet nothing until the payload
  itself actually changes. Adding a stamp would mark all 30 current copies
  hand-modified and force a no-op PR into every repo.
